#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055901} :activity/id #uuid "9909dcbb-b89b-5206-8845-506fff77b2d7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055927 :tutorial/content "We're now able to prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( \\ln   ( x ) ) = \\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we set \n\n$$\ny = \\ln   ( x ) .\n$$ \nOur goal is to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, note that since $y = \\ln   ( x ) ,$ we have\n\n$$\ne^y = x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x}} ( {e}^{y} ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x}} ( x ) \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} y}} ( {e}^{y} ) & \\displaystyle  = 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} \\cdot  {e}^{y} & \\displaystyle  = 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {e}^{y}}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $e^y = x,$ we conclude\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac1x\n$$\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "703b0ae9-246b-57d0-a0af-5bee4dbafe4a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10531 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Natural Logarithm"} :step/id #uuid "d055b170-c1bf-5590-a0c3-e130bae66abb" :step/math-academy-id 210843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054572 :question/id #uuid "af328f52-53a1-5cc1-a542-6cad8eea4c44" :question/math-academy-id "e-6114" :question/problem "Given $xy+y^2=x^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ (x+2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711283} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711369}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"} :step/id #uuid "dce98779-99bc-5774-beef-a23e45a94a72" :step/math-academy-id 22699 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055989 :tutorial/content "Functions are usually given in the form $y=f(x)$ where $y$ is explicitly written as a function of $x.$ However, they can also be written in **implicit** form, like\n$$\n2x^3+y^2=3.\n$$\n\nWhen a function is given in implicit form, we can differentiate it using the method of **implicit differentiation**, which consists of the following two steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x.$\n\nWe need to remember that $y$ *is a function of* $x,$ i.e., $y = y(x),$ So, we apply the chain rule:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 2 {x}^{3} + {y}^{2} ) &  = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 3 ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 2 {x}^{3} ) + \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {y}^{2} ) &  = 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( 2 {x}^{3} ) + \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} y} ( {y}^{2} ) ( \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} ) &  = 0 \\\\ 6 {x}^{2} + 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 6 {x}^{2} + 2 y {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 y {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = - 6 {x}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}} & \\displaystyle  = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 {x}^{2}}{y}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the derivative of $y$ is $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=- \\dfrac{3x^2}{y}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4f8a0764-c99e-5be6-8b49-3ca28725caf9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 433 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "47c11c73-2f53-5115-996d-e1dca896a32a" :step/math-academy-id 806 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054503 :question/id #uuid "59d2e907-e788-5a22-93a9-c49ff94df6ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-190" :question/problem "Given ${x}^{2} + {y}^{2} = \\sin   ( y ) ,$ find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {x}^{2} + {y}^{2} ) &  = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( \\sin   ( y ) ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {x}^{2} ) + \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( {y}^{2} ) &  = \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} x} ( \\sin   ( y ) ) \\\\ 2 x + 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = \\cos   ( y ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}2 x + 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = \\cos   ( y ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} \\\\ 2 y \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} - \\cos   ( y ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = - 2 x \\\\ ( 2 y - \\cos   ( y ) ) \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = - 2 x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = - \\frac{2 x}{2 y - \\cos   ( y )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} &  = \\frac{2 x}{\\cos   ( y ) - 2 y}\\end{array}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bf25f86-6b27-5eaa-b1a1-1104478b290a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711319} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711421}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form"} :step/id #uuid "df7753ed-cc52-5fcd-b5cf-d0d376cf15e6" :step/math-academy-id 807 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054504 :question/id #uuid "8a6eb51f-6232-5a08-acf1-136f0d14a4d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-191" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ for the curve $xy+y^2=2x^2+4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x \\\\ (x+2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(\\dfrac{4x-y}{x+2y}\\right)_{\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{4\\cdot 1-2}{1+2\\cdot 2}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{2}{5} \\, .\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2311a895-587e-5609-8a9f-9963b8cae372" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711343} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711396}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "ae453d7c-abe1-5c23-af16-160d6d1d7d2a" :step/math-academy-id 810 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054505 :question/id #uuid "cd630daa-4813-5ca8-b3d4-5fe35c4af4fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-192" :question/problem "The equation $x^2-2x+y^2+4y=5$ represents a circle. The normal line at the point $\\left(0, -5 \\right)$ on the circle intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c52a76654a4a545d356890d4deb04c74301e4091422a51e84551aeb3ec1152.png)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(0,-5).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, $m',$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\nm' = -\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y = y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2-2x+ y^2+4y)\n\t&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5)\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (x^2)-\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (2x)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (y^2)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (4y)&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (5)\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t2x-2+2y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}+4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=0\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\n\t(2y+4)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=2-2x\\\\[5pt]\t\n\t\n\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\t&=\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\n\t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\tm\n\t&=\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\left(\\dfrac{2-2x}{2y+4}\\right)_{\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{2-2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot (-5)+4}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=\\dfrac{2}{-6}\\\\[5pt]\n\t&=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\, .\n\t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is $m'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=3.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(0,\\,-5 \\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\ty-y_1 &=m'\\left(x-x_1 \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\ty+5 &=3\\left(x-0 \\right)\\\\[5pt]\n\ty&=3x-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\ty&=3x-5\\\\[5pt]\n\t0&=3x-5\\\\[5pt]\n\tx&=\\dfrac{5}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3},\\,0 \\right).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049119}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711771} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711812}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"} :step/id #uuid "73b8ac60-1c84-5c86-a80d-cef53c382fb9" :step/math-academy-id 814 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055635}}] :activity/title "Implicit Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054912}]}
